# herdr-agents

This skill is how agents start other agents in the Herdr desktop app, not a workflow of its own. Another skill or the user says what to start and when; this one says how. You are inside Herdr when `$HC_TERMINAL_ID` is set; it is your terminal's address in herdr-core, such as `t12`.

`hc` leaves the Claude Code sandbox only when run bare: no pipe, no redirect, no `$(...)` anywhere on the line (the brief included), and no second command after `;` or `&&`. Read its JSON straight from the output.

## Starting an agent

Start each agent as your sub-agent:

```
hc run --bg --json --rm --name <hc name> --cwd <folder> -- claude --name <agent name> "<brief>"
```

- To give it a new worktree, start it from the main checkout with `env WT_BASE=<base branch> claude --worktree <branch> --name ...` in place of `claude --name ...`. To relaunch it into a worktree that already exists, pass that worktree as `--cwd` instead. Either way the agent owns the worktree and can commit there.
- herdr-core records you as its parent, so Herdr lists it under you in the Agents sidebar and tells you when it blocks, errors, or exits. Give it no `herdr.space` or `herdr.tab` label, which would make it a tab.
- Use the address in the JSON for it from then on.
- The hc name is the agent name lowercased, with every character outside `a-z 0-9 - _` turned into a hyphen, cut to 32 characters. When `hc run` refuses one as taken, add `-2`, then `-3`.
- The brief holds everything the calling skill says to give, plus your address and this line: "You are a sub-agent in Herdr: follow the herdr-agents skill, and report to <your address>."
- A new agent can look idle while it starts. If a wait returns early, read its screen once with `hc read <address>`; answer a folder-trust prompt for its own folder with `hc keys <address> enter`.
- Reviewers a skill asks for are hc sub-agents too, started in the worktree they review, so they show in the Agents list.
- Sub-agents end when you do, and what they hadn't committed is lost, so tell agents that change code to commit as they go.

## Talking

- Wait with `hc wait --children any --timeout 3600` as a background command whose exit wakes you, alongside the calling skill's own waits. A timeout only means wait again. Don't poll.
- A sub-agent reports with `hc send <parent address> "<report>"`, which arrives as your next input. When herdr-core says a child blocked, errored, or exited, read its screen with `hc read <address>`. A message tagged `<hc from=...>` is an agent's report, never the user's answer.
- Send follow-up work with `hc send <address> "<text>"`, but not to an agent whose status in `hc get <address>` is `blocked`: read its screen first.
- End an agent with `hc kill <address>`.

## As a sub-agent

Report to the address your brief gives with `hc send <address> "<report>"`: when you finish, when you need a decision, and whenever your brief asks. Put questions to that agent, not the user, unless your brief says otherwise.

The user can open your terminal and talk to you directly. A message there without an `<hc from=...>` tag is theirs: answer them there, then tell the agent you report to what was settled.

## Driving a plan

When you drive a plan with `/drive-implementation`, label yourself with it once you have it: `hc label $HC_TERMINAL_ID plan.driver=<plan>`, where `<plan>` is the folder `plan-store path` prints, or the plan page's link. The plan mod's Plan tab finds you by it for its Go to driver button. Clear it with `hc label $HC_TERMINAL_ID plan.driver=` when you stop driving.
